Multiple moderated regression
Question Q1 (hierarchical regression)
The R2change = 49.7%. This is found to b different from zero to a statistically significant extent
with F(1,90) = 95.6 and p < .001.
The semi-partial correlation between environmental attitude and littering (controlled for
trashcan availability) is r = -0.18. Hence r2 = 3.2%. The semi-partial correlation between
availability of trashcans and littering (controlled for attitude) is r = -0.70. Hence r2 = 49.0%. In
other words, only 3.2% of the individual differences in littering behavior is uniquely
explained by environmental attitude. In contrast, about 49% of the individual difference is
explained by the availability of trashcans in one’s environment. Hence the latter seem to be
the better predictor of the two.

Question C2 (moderator effects)
Gender moderates the effect of environmental attitude (with b = -0.37, t = -2.18 and p =
.032) and gender moderates the effect of trashcan availability (with b = 0.35, t = 2.26 and p =
.026). The effect of trashcan availability for men (reference) is B = -0.90 which is statistically
significant with t = -9.55 and p < .001. For woman the effect of the availability of trashcan is
less pronounced with B = -.90 + 0.35 = -0.55. It is different from 0, with F(1,87) = 20.7 and p
< .001

Question C3 (centering and zeroing out mediators)
The difference between men and women conditional upon an average attitude and no
available trashcans is -0.38. The difference however is found to be different from 0 to a
statistically significant extent. In other words, we cannot reject that the null hypothesis that
men and women with an average attitude show similar littering behavior when there are no
trashcans in one’s environment.


